\section{Introduction}
Aoheng (Penihing, ISO 639-3 pni) is a Kayan-group Austronesian language of East Kalimantan, Indonesia, spoken along the upper Mahakam around Long Apari and Tiong Bu'u. The figure of about 2,600 speakers dates from 1981 and may be outdated. The searches found almost no educational material; what exists is academic.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Aoheng were found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Overview article: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aoheng_language}
\item Numeral system entry (Max Planck Institute): \url{https://lingweb.eva.mpg.de/channumerals/Aoheng.htm}
\item Sellato, \emph{Menggenggam Dunia: Mengukur dan Mengira dalam Kalangan Masyarakat Aoheng Borneo} (measuring and counting among the Aoheng): \url{https://publisher.unimas.my/ojs/index.php/BJK/article/view/4399}
\item Glottolog reference record, Sellato and Soriente 2015: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/472369}
\item Blust and Smith, bibliography of the languages of Borneo: \url{https://muse.jhu.edu/article/586718}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
No scripture translations or community organisations were found in the searches.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video or corpus resources were found in the searches.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is still a first language for all adults but not all young people, and is not known to be taught in schools. Any curriculum would need new documentation work with the community. The Sellato material on traditional measurement and the Blust--Smith bibliography are possible starting points for locating further sources.

\end{document}
